In article <1991Apr15.104915.8707@gdr.bath.ac.uk> exspes@gdr.bath.ac.uk (P E Smee) writes:

>While I go for the idea of 'controlling' polypiling in some way, I
>would hate to see it eliminated.

Same here. I previously liked the proposal to make polymorphed wands of wishing
have only 1 or 2 charges left. I no longer like the idea. It is an artificial
way to limit the player. No such ways should be added to the game.

A better way is to add dangers into the act of (formerly safe) polypiling.
The player can still choose to polypile, but only if he accepts the risks,
                                                        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
like turning a pile into a weapons/leather/trash/dead golem. And the addition
of wands of everything (and spellbooks of lost spells etc.) is quite a nice
addition too. (But not as nice as golems.)

>there should be some other mechanism added to the game to ensure that
>required items would be generated somewhere.

How do you define 'required items'? Right now I can't think of an item,
that you can't win without.

>(I.e., the
>game should always be possible to win, IF played correctly.  My
>propensity for getting myself killed is sufficiently great that I don't
>really need help in the form of impossible games.)

I would bet that this problem is either unsolvable or NP-complete and
thus unsolvable in any reasonable time. (Sorry, but I'm having a test
tomorrow on the theory of computation..:)

One thing that could be done is that there be at least N items of a given
type in a game. What I specifically have in mind is to insure that there
be at least 5 shops in a game.
